### What is the problem?

Design a class that is initialized with a value K and an initial list of numbers. It supports an `add` operation that inserts a new number and returns the Kth largest element among all numbers seen so far.

```java
package com.bank.sort;

import java.util.PriorityQueue;

public class KthLargestStream {

    private final int k;
    private final PriorityQueue<Integer> minHeap;

    public KthLargestStream(int k, int[] nums) {
        this.k = k;
        this.minHeap = new PriorityQueue<>();
        for (int num : nums) {
            add(num);
        }
    }

    public int add(int val) {
        minHeap.offer(val);
        if (minHeap.size() > k) {
            minHeap.poll();  // remove the smallest
        }
        return minHeap.peek();   // root is the Kth largest
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] initial = {40000, 50000, 20000, 80000, 10000};
        KthLargestStream stream = new KthLargestStream(3, initial);
        // K=3, initial heap after seeding: [40000, 50000, 80000]

        System.out.println("Add 30000: " + stream.add(30000)); // 40000
        System.out.println("Add 60000: " + stream.add(60000)); // 50000
        System.out.println("Add 90000: " + stream.add(90000)); // 60000
    }
}
```

```text
Add 30000: 40000
Add 60000: 50000
Add 90000: 60000
```

### Why does a min-heap of size K work?

The heap always holds exactly the K largest values seen. The root is the smallest of these K, which is the Kth largest overall. When a new value arrives, if it is larger than the root it should enter the top-K, pushing out the current root. The offer/poll logic handles this automatically.

```java
package com.bank.sort;

import java.util.PriorityQueue;

public class WhyMinHeap {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        PriorityQueue<Integer> heap = new PriorityQueue<>();
        int k = 3;
        int[] stream = {50, 80, 20, 40, 90, 10, 70};

        for (int val : stream) {
            heap.offer(val);
            if (heap.size() > k) heap.poll();
            System.out.println("Add " + val + ": heap=" + heap
                + " Kth=" + heap.peek());
        }
    }
}
```

```text
Add 50: heap=[50] Kth=50
Add 80: heap=[50, 80] Kth=50
Add 20: heap=[20, 80, 50] Kth=20
Add 40: heap=[40, 80, 50] Kth=40
Add 90: heap=[50, 90, 80] Kth=50
Add 10: heap=[50, 90, 80] Kth=50
Add 70: heap=[70, 90, 80] Kth=70
```

### How does the constructor seed the heap?

The constructor feeds the initial numbers through the same `add` logic. This ensures the heap settles into holding the K largest from the start.

```java
package com.bank.sort;

import java.util.PriorityQueue;

public class ConstructorSeeding {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        PriorityQueue<Integer> heap = new PriorityQueue<>();
        int k = 3;
        int[] initial = {50000, 20000, 80000, 10000, 40000};

        for (int num : initial) {
            heap.offer(num);
            if (heap.size() > k) heap.poll();
        }
        System.out.println("Heap after seeding: " + heap);
        System.out.println("Kth largest: " + heap.peek());
    }
}
```

```text
Heap after seeding: [40000, 80000, 50000]
Kth largest: 40000
```

### What happens when fewer than K values have been seen?

When fewer than K values exist, the heap holds all of them and its root is the smallest of whatever has been seen. This is the correct Kth largest for the current count, or you can define it as the smallest value.

```java
package com.bank.sort;

import java.util.PriorityQueue;

public class FewerThanK {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        PriorityQueue<Integer> heap = new PriorityQueue<>();
        int k = 5;
        int[] values = {30, 10, 20};

        for (int v : values) {
            heap.offer(v);
            if (heap.size() > k) heap.poll();
            System.out.println("Add " + v + ": size=" + heap.size()
                + " root=" + heap.peek());
        }
    }
}
```

```text
Add 30: size=1 root=30
Add 10: size=2 root=10
Add 20: size=3 root=10
```

### What is the time and space complexity?

| Operation | Time | Space |
|---|---|---|
| Constructor | O(n log K) for n initial elements | O(K) |
| add | O(log K) | O(K) |
| peek (query) | O(1) | O(K) |

### How is this different from sorting the entire stream?

Sorting the entire stream to find the Kth largest would take O(n log n) per query and O(n) memory. The heap approach answers each add in O(log K) and uses only O(K) memory, which is bounded regardless of stream length.

| Approach | Time per add | Memory |
|---|---|---|
| Sort all | O(n log n) | O(n) |
| Min-heap K | O(log K) | O(K) |
| Quickselect | O(n) avg | O(n) |

### How is this used in a banking system?

At the National Bank of India, a fraud dashboard shows the Kth largest transaction amount as a live high-value watermark that updates with every incoming transaction. The size-K min-heap lets the dashboard respond to each new amount in O(log K) time while holding only K values.

```java
package com.bank.sort;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.PriorityQueue;

public class FraudDashboard {

    private final int k;
    private final PriorityQueue<BigDecimal> heap;

    public FraudDashboard(int k) {
        this.k = k;
        this.heap = new PriorityQueue<>();
    }

    public BigDecimal addTransaction(BigDecimal amount) {
        heap.offer(amount);
        if (heap.size() > k) heap.poll();
        return heap.peek();
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        FraudDashboard dashboard = new FraudDashboard(3);
        String[] txns = {"50000", "80000", "20000", "90000", "60000"};
        for (String t : txns) {
            BigDecimal amt = new BigDecimal(t);
            BigDecimal kth = dashboard.addTransaction(amt);
            System.out.println("Txn " + t + " -> 3rd largest: " + kth);
        }
    }
}
```

```text
Txn 50000 -> 3rd largest: 50000
Txn 80000 -> 3rd largest: 50000
Txn 20000 -> 3rd largest: 20000
Txn 90000 -> 3rd largest: 50000
Txn 60000 -> 3rd largest: 60000
```

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

Interviewers often extend this problem:
- What if K changes dynamically? (Adjust the heap size.)
- How do you find both Kth largest and Kth smallest? (Two heaps.)
- How does this relate to the median-finding problem? (Two heaps: max-heap for lower half, min-heap for upper half.)
